comments.commentText:
Re: Docket No. ICEB-2026-0100, Optional Practical Training Fees (RIN 1653-AB01)

The proposed $70,000 initial and $30,000 subsequent OPT fees would reverse the economics that make international enrollment a net benefit to American students and institutions. The proposal's analysis does not account for this. DHS should withdraw the proposal.

1. International students are net financial contributors. F-1 students are ineligible for federal student aid. According to Open Doors, 55% of international students' primary funding comes from personal and family sources. At public four-year institutions, international students generally pay out-of-state tuition, which averaged $31,880 in 2025–26, against $11,950 for in-state students (College Board). Generous need-based aid for international undergraduates is rare: only six U.S. colleges admit them need-blind, and at those schools they make up roughly a tenth of undergraduates. Estimates by Clemens (2026), as summarized by the Institute for Progress, put universities' net revenue at roughly $13,700 a year per international master's student and about $23,000 per international bachelor's student.

2. That revenue supports domestic enrollment. Shih (2017, Journal of Public Economics) studied graduate enrollment from 1995 to 2005. He found that every 10 additional international students were associated with about 8 additional domestic students. The effect was driven by full-paying master's students, whose net tuition cross-subsidizes domestic seats. The pattern matters most in fields where international students are a large share of enrollment. In computer science master's programs, nonresident students made up about 53% of enrollment in 2025 (CRA Taulbee Survey).

3. OPT is central to the decision to enroll. Research summarized by IFP finds that 82% of international STEM graduates who remain in the United States use OPT before moving to H-1B. In a 2026 IFP survey of 734 current and former international students, 70% of STEM-eligible respondents said they would not have enrolled in a U.S. program had fees like these been in place. Survey responses are not behavior, but the direction matches economic estimates. Clemens (2026) projects that the fee would cut OPT participation by 23% to 99% and international master's enrollment by 5% to 58%, depending on who bears it.

4. The proposal's analysis omits these effects. DHS acknowledges that the fee could affect international enrollment and that schools may pass it on to students or employers. According to IFP's review, however, the proposal's estimates assume international enrollment does not change. The analysis therefore counts fee revenue of $8.4 to $16.5 billion a year without counting:
• tuition revenue lost when students choose other countries;
• the resulting reduction in domestic seats that this revenue supports;
• the disproportionate effect on smaller institutions, which DHS says are 56% of SEVP-certified schools, and for which it rejected an exemption.
An agency must consider "an important aspect of the problem" (Motor Vehicle Mfrs. Ass'n v. State Farm, 463 U.S. 29 (1983)). The effect of a six-figure fee on enrollment is such an aspect.

5. Reliance interests are dismissed, not weighed. OPT has existed for decades, and the D.C. Circuit upheld it in Washington Alliance of Technology Workers v. DHS (2022). Prospective students choose programs, take on costs and plan careers relying on it. The proposal's answer, that the F-1 classification "was never intended to provide a pathway to employment," is a policy view, not an assessment of reliance. DHS v. Regents of the University of California, 591 U.S. 1 (2020), requires DHS to assess whether such interests exist, weigh them, and consider alternatives that would accommodate them.

6. Recommendation. DHS should withdraw the proposed fees. If it proceeds in any form, it should:
• quantify the effect on international enrollment and on tuition revenue, including the effect on domestic enrollment;
• analyze small-entity impacts and adopt the small-entity exemption it rejected;
• address fraud through employer-focused measures rather than a charge on every student;
• reopen comment for at least 60 days, as Executive Order 12866 recommends for significant rules.
